{{define "content"}}
<vox-hero eyebrow="OpenVox Console" heading="Your whole fleet, in one place">
  <p>
    See every node, decide what runs where, deploy your code, and act on
    what comes back. A Puppet Enterprise console for OpenVox &mdash; one
    Go binary, on top of the openvox-server and openvoxdb you already run.
  </p>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="#capabilities">See what it does</vox-cta>
  <vox-cta slot="actions" href="https://github.com/avitacco/openvox-console" target="_blank">Get it on GitHub</vox-cta>
</vox-hero>

{{screenshot "dashboard"}}

<vox-grid min="200px" gap="lg">
  <vox-stat value="1" label="binary to deploy"></vox-stat>
  <vox-stat value="2" label="architectures, built on every push"></vox-stat>
  <vox-stat value="GPL-3.0" label="free and open source"></vox-stat>
</vox-grid>

<section class="site-section" id="capabilities">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl">What it does</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p>
      Every page below shows the console itself, captured from a running
      instance.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-link-hub>
  <vox-link-hub-item href="features/nodes.html" heading="Nodes and inventory">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="plug"></vox-icon>
    Every node, what it last reported, whether it is reachable, and the
    state of its certificate.
  </vox-link-hub-item>

  <vox-link-hub-item href="features/classification.html" heading="Classification">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="node-group"></vox-icon>
    Rule-based node groups that decide what configuration applies where.
  </vox-link-hub-item>

  <vox-link-hub-item href="features/code.html" heading="Code deployment">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="deploy"></vox-icon>
    Ship Puppet code from one control repository or many, on demand or by
    webhook.
  </vox-link-hub-item>

  <vox-link-hub-item href="features/orchestration.html" heading="Orchestration">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="orchestrate"></vox-icon>
    Run Puppet, tasks and plans across the fleet, and see what each node
    did.
  </vox-link-hub-item>

  <vox-link-hub-item href="features/security.html" heading="Packages and vulnerabilities">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="vulnerability"></vox-icon>
    What is installed everywhere, and which advisories affect it.
  </vox-link-hub-item>

  <vox-link-hub-item href="features/access-control.html" heading="Access control and audit">
    <vox-icon slot="icon" name="people"></vox-icon>
    Roles, service tokens, and a record of who changed what.
  </vox-link-hub-item>
</vox-link-hub>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl">One binary to run</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p>
      The console, access control, classification, orchestration and code
      deployment are one process. There is nothing to wire together, no
      internal services to keep in step, and one thing to upgrade.
    </p>
    <p>
      It sits on top of the stack you already have. openvox-server still
      compiles catalogs and runs the CA; openvoxdb still stores facts and
      reports. Your agents carry on talking to exactly what they talk to
      now &mdash; the console serves the standard ENC contract, so there
      is nothing to change on the node side.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<vox-callout variant="tip" heading="Try it on a throwaway stack first">
  <p>
    <code>docker compose up</code> brings up openvoxserver, openvoxdb,
    Postgres and the console together, so you can point a test agent at it
    and see your own nodes appear before committing to anything.
  </p>
</vox-callout>
{{end}}
